message.content:
{
  "text": "Plants absorb water through their roots and release some of it as vapor through leaf openings called stomata. This release is transpiration. Evapotranspiration combines evaporation from surfaces with transpiration by plants. When air cools sufficiently and becomes saturated, vapor can condense onto small particles to form droplets. Clouds contain tiny liquid droplets, ice crystals, or both; they are not simply invisible water vapor.",
  "reason": "The text was reconstructed by sequentially ordering the provided claims to maintain logical flow: starting with plant water uptake (roots), moving to release mechanisms (stomata/transpiration), defining the combined process (evapotranspiration), explaining condensation conditions (cooling/saturation), and concluding with cloud composition. Background relations were excluded as per instructions, and no facts were invented."
}

